    <div class="post-container" id="fpc-2037732">
                      
		<div class="post" id="post-2037732">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2037732)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2037732">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser deleted" data-id="1361261"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/common--images/avatars/default/a16.png" alt=""/>(account deleted)</span> <span class="odate time_1400875539 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">23 May 2014 20:05</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2037732">
									

<p>Neutral vote. On the positive side, this is, for the most part, well-written stylistically, and explores characters (mostly Xyank) in a way that is consistent<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-589811-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-589811-1')">1</a></sup> with previous works, while at the same time adding genuine depth and not merely extending a stereotype. The action in the story held my interest, and I particularly enjoyed the sequence in the temporally-unstable deli.</p>
<p>However, the farther this series goes, the more my structural issues with it are entrenched. Cain had some comments on the hub page about the awkwardness of constantly introducing and re-introducing characters, and I believe he has a point. There's something about the way people are introduced, in this tale and others, that is jarring to read in terms of flow. Generally speaking, I prefer when characters' identities and personalities are allowed to unfold in the course of a story, and in this and other tales I'm getting essentially a mini-dossier on people like your junior researcher, which to me interrupts the story. That may be more a function of how Cavalcade is structured than it is your writing in particular, but I can't help but feel that there must be a more effective way of handling the continued entry of new characters, stylistically.</p>
<p>Also, and this is more a personal thing than anything else at this point, I find myself frustrated when reading the tales that I like more in this series by the fact that, by definition, the person writing each installment cannot know which way the story is headed. It basically means that if something is headed in a direction that I'm excited about, I'm all but guaranteed to be disappointed when someone takes the story to an entirely different place (one that more often than not it seems is a non-sequitur to the rest of the action), through no real fault of their own I might add.</p>
<p>This is a fine effort, but I'm afraid the larger context in which it is appearing is doing the piece a disservice.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-589811-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-589811-1')">1</a>. Note that this is in no way an endorsement of &quot;consistency with previous works&quot; as a general standard of evaluation, but it is something you seem to be going for here.</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
					
